#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275231 :question/problem "The closed curve $C$ is defined as $\\mathbf{r}(t)=7\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+9\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector for $C$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=\\frac{a(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+b(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}}{||{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)||}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $a(t).$\n\n*Hint: Observe that $C$ is traversed clockwise.*" :question/worked-solution "For convenience, we express $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}7\\operatorname{sin}t \\\\ 9\\operatorname{cos}t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward normal vector of a closed, *negatively* oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{'}(t) \\\\ {y}^{'}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}7\\operatorname{cos}t \\\\ -9\\operatorname{sin}t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented clockwise, we rotate ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *counterclockwise* to obtain an outward normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise rotation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 7\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{n} & =\\frac{1}{||{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)||}\\begin{bmatrix}9\\operatorname{sin}t \\\\ 7\\operatorname{cos}t\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{9\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+7\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}}{||{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $a(t)=9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275232 :question/problem "The closed curve $C$ is defined as $\\mathbf{r}(t)=5\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector for $C$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=\\frac{a(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+b(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}}{||{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)||}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $a(t).$\n\n*Hint: Observe that $C$ is traversed clockwise.*" :question/worked-solution "For convenience, we express $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5\\operatorname{sin}t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, *negatively* oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{'}(t) \\\\ {y}^{'}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5\\operatorname{cos}t \\\\ -3\\operatorname{sin}t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented clockwise, we rotate ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *counterclockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise rotation is\n\n$$\nR=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{n} & =\\frac{1}{||{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)||}\\begin{bmatrix}3\\operatorname{sin}t \\\\ 5\\operatorname{cos}t\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+5\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}}{||{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $a(t)=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275233 :question/problem "The closed curve $C$ is defined as $\\mathbf{r}(t)=9\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+4\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector for $C$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=\\frac{a(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+b(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}}{||{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)||}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $b(t).$\n\n*Hint: Observe that $C$ is traversed clockwise.*" :question/worked-solution "For convenience, we express $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}9\\operatorname{sin}t \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cos}t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward normal vector of a closed, *negatively* oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{'}(t) \\\\ {y}^{'}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}9\\operatorname{cos}t \\\\ -4\\operatorname{sin}t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented clockwise, we rotate ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ *counterclockwise* to obtain an outward normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise rotation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nRotating the vector ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ by ${90}^{∘}$ counterclockwise, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}9\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 9\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{n} & =\\frac{1}{||{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)||}\\begin{bmatrix}4\\operatorname{sin}t \\\\ 9\\operatorname{cos}t\\end{bmatrix}=\\frac{4\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+9\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}}{||{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)||}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $b(t)=9\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275236 :question/problem "![](images/28/2860103dc3d1019cddcffa504b3cce1fb8a84cfd046bc796712d1e16516d5269.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}4y{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the section of the curve $\\frac{x}{2}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$ traversed between the points $(0,1)$ and $(2,e),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve can be expressed in the form $x=x(y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{2}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\,⟹\\,x=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\n$$\n\nTherefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{ln}t,\\hspace{0.222em}t⟩,\\hspace{2em}1\\le t\\le e\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1\\le t\\le e$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{ln}t)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{2}{t}\\right)}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{{t}^{2}}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=4y{e}^{2x}$, then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =4y{e}^{2x} \\\\  & =4t{e}^{4\\operatorname{ln}t} \\\\  & =4t({t}^{4}) \\\\  & =4{t}^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}4y{e}^{2x}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}(4{t}^{5})\\cdot \\frac{1}{t}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\color{blue}{t}^{4}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275240 :question/problem "![](images/8d/8d060e257d74e6e0e6b7f35bfb89e9e974499059ddf4d029c0cace195ba67c13.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\frac{1}{y-x}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\nwhere $C$ is the segment of the line $x=2y-4$ from $(0,2)$ to $(2,3),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y),$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=x(t)=2t-4.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨2t-4,\\hspace{0.222em}t⟩,\\hspace{2em}2\\le t\\le 3\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $2\\le t\\le 3$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t-4)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(2)}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{y-x},$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =\\frac{1}{t-(2t-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{1}{y-x}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{4-t}\\cdot \\sqrt{5}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{4-t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve this using the substitution $u=4-t,$ $\\text{d}u=-\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{4-t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{2}^{1}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot (-\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{2}^{1}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡u]_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1] \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-0] \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275244 :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}6{x}^{6}{e}^{-2y}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x={e}^{y/2}$ from $(1,0)$ to $(\\sqrt{2},\\operatorname{ln}⁡2).$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y),$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=x(t)={e}^{t/2}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨{e}^{t/2},\\hspace{0.222em}t⟩,\\hspace{2em}0\\le t\\le \\operatorname{ln}2\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $0\\le t\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡2$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t/2})\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{t/2}}{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{{e}^{t/2}}{2}\\right)}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{e}^{t}}{4}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{{e}^{t}+4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=6{x}^{6}{e}^{-2y}$, then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =6{x}^{6}{e}^{-2y} \\\\  & =6{({e}^{t/2})}^{6}{e}^{-2t} \\\\  & =6{e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}6{x}^{6}{e}^{-2y}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}2}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}2}6{e}^{t}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{{e}^{t}+4}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}2}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{{e}^{t}+4}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve this using the substitution $u={e}^{t}+4,$ $\\text{d}u={e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{{e}^{t}+4}\\,\\text{d}t & =3{\\int }_{5}^{6}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}\\,{|}_{5}^{6} \\\\  & =2({6}^{3/2}-{5}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =2(6\\sqrt{6}-5\\sqrt{5}) \\\\  & =12\\sqrt{6}-10\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275256 :question/problem "![](images/0d/0d2aa60719643235f5ae684d59607199731626e15bac40fd6b14d071c099ab4a.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ shown above, and let $C$ be the path along the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Remember that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ gives the total work done by a force $\\mathbf{F}$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ is false, while statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0\n$$ is true. It's clear from the picture below that $\\mathbf{F}$  works against the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B.$\n\n![](images/2b/2b8ef08638551571caa43150080a8550a0b61ed24eaa7c6da73fe66edd161e8e.png)\n\n \nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ is true. It's clear from the picture below that if we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then $\\mathbf{F}$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves from $B$ to $A.$\n\n![](images/28/28db5c349d3455c58f81da7c181586ba4e0ad5662f35faa2575d6622b3713988.png)\n\n \nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0;\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275259 :question/problem "![](images/67/678ab825415e107bca9554d90e2ad5a4d8cc90a5ef68d817972ef98dfc165922.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)$ shown above, and let $C$ be the path along the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Remember that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ gives the total work done by a force $\\mathbf{F}$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion  of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ is false, while statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$ is true. Since the force $\\mathbf{F}$ acts perpendicular to the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B,$ it neither helps nor hinders its motion. \n\n![](images/0e/0e8d0fcf619987f49869cc6ae9eb5cb0c7acbbb23f45ce8f64626dfd1debdc63.png)\n\n \nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0\n$$ is false. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then the situation with regards to the work done by $\\mathbf{F}$ is similar to the previous case.\n\n![](images/b2/b2bec1f1da172b623f20d28fd0e9c5a248cf00dc621769851ca33b3b545b8592.png)\n\n \nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275260 :question/problem "Which of the following gives the arc length of the polar curve $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta )$ for $$\n\\displaystyle -\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{6}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta ),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\theta ).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}\\sqrt{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta ))^{2}+(-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\theta ))^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}\\sqrt{4{cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )+36{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}\\sqrt{4({cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )+9{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta ))}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )+9{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275261 :question/problem "![](images/59/59214bcbfd4b1d718faa0c2355e932ab3cedae5b785b17497cef554ee3ec099b.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the arc length of the polar curve $r=3-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta )$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}$ (shown in blue in the diagram above)." :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=3-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta ),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\theta ).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{(3-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta ))^{2}+(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\theta ))^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{9-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta )+{cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )+9{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{9-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta )+1+8{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{10-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta )+8{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275262 :question/problem "The equation of a curve is ${x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}=6.$ The normal line at the point $(-3,\\,1)$ on the curve intersects the $x$-axis at the point $P.$ What are the coordinates of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we need to find the slope of the normal line at the point $(-3,\\,1).$  Remember that the slope of the normal line, ${m}^{′},$ is the negative reciprocal of the slope of the tangent line:\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we will calculate the derivative using implicit differentiation, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{y}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ 2x-6y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ -6y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{-2x}{-6y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{x}{3y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at the point $(-3,\\,1)$ is \n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}_{(-3,\\,1)} \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{x}{3y}\\right)}_{(-3,\\,1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{3\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line is ${m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{-1}=1.$ We can write the equation of the normal at the point $(-3,\\,1)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-{y}_{1}) & ={m}^{′}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ (y-1) & =1(x+3) \\\\ y & =(x+3)+1 \\\\ y & =x+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to find where this normal line intersects the $x$-axis. At the point $P$ the $y$-coordinate is $0,$ so we set $y=0$ and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x+4 \\\\ 0 & =x+4 \\\\ x & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(-4,\\,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275263 :question/problem "![](images/00/00e5e0f5c47a056beaca2d7bc1b1d10a6a11f96398df7400e69c023f5780b8a6.png)\n\nWhich of the following integrals gives the arc length of the polar curve $r=1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )$ for $$\n\\displaystyle -\\,\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}\n$$ (shown in blue in the  diagram above)?" :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))^{2}+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{1+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )+4{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{1+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+1+3{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{2+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+3{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275264 :question/problem "Which of the following gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$$\n\\displaystyle x=t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t),\\,y=1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\n$$\nfrom $t=2$ to $t=5$ ?" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))=1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{5}\\sqrt{(1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}+(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{5}\\sqrt{1+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+4{cos}^{2}⁡(t)+4{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)}\\,\\text{d}t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275327 :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{sin}(2t),\\hspace{1em}y={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}(t),\\hspace{2em}0\\le t<2\\pi ,\n$$\n\ncalculate the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\,{y}^{′}(t)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\n \n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ the slope of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =-\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275332 :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx={e}^{1/t},\\,y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-1}},\\,1<t<\\infty ,\n$$\n\ncalculate the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=2.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}{e}^{1/t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=-\\frac{t}{({t}^{2}-1)^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{t}{({t}^{2}-1)^{3/2}}\\right)}{(-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}{e}^{1/t})} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{3}{e}^{-1/t}}{({t}^{2}-1)^{3/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=2,$ the slope of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{2}^{3}{e}^{-1/2}}{({2}^{2}-1)^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{{3}^{3/2}\\sqrt{e}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{27e}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275333 :question/problem "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡({e}^{t})},\\,y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡({e}^{t}),\\,-\\infty <t<1,\n$$\n\ncalculate the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}({e}^{t})}}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}({e}^{t})\\cdot {e}^{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}({e}^{t})}{2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}({e}^{t})}}, \\\\ {y}^{'}(t) & =-{e}^{t}{\\operatorname{csc}}^{2}({e}^{t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} & =\\frac{-{e}^{t}{\\operatorname{csc}}^{2}({e}^{t})}{\\left(\\frac{{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}({e}^{t})}{2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}({e}^{t})}}\\right)} \\\\  & =-\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}({e}^{t})}}{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}({e}^{t})\\operatorname{cos}({e}^{t})} \\\\  & =-\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2}{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3/2}({e}^{t})\\operatorname{cos}({e}^{t})}\\hspace{0.167em}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right),$ the slope of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} & =-\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2}{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3/2}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)} \\\\  & =-\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2}{{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{3/2}\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {2}^{3/2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =-8\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}\\hspace{0.167em}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275337 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\frac{1}{3},\\,y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ on the curve. Determine the point at which the tangent to the curve at $C$ crosses the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+\\frac{1}{3}=1+0+\\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{4}{3}, \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+1=0+1=1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the tangent line to the curve at the point $(\\frac{4}{3},1).$ \n\nTo find the equation of the tangent line, we need to find $\\frac{dy}{dx}.$ To compute it, we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\left(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=\\pi /2} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1-0} \\\\  & =1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the tangent line, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(\\frac{4}{3},1)$ and the slope $m=1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1 & =1\\cdot (x-\\frac{4}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe tangent crosses the $x$-axis when $y=0.$ At this point, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0-1 & =1\\cdot (x-\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\ -1 & =x-\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $(\\frac{1}{3},\\,0)$ is the $x$-intercept."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275353 :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically by\n\n$$\nx=5-t,\\,\\,\\,y={t}^{2}-1,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=5-t\\,⇒\\,t=5-x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=5-x$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(5-x)^{2}-1 \\\\  & =25-10x+{x}^{2}-1 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-10x+24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y={x}^{2}-10x+24.$\n\nFinally, since $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $x=5-t,$ we must have $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSo, the complete Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\ny={x}^{2}-10x+24,\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275354 :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically by\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{t},\\,\\,\\,y=t(9-t),\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\sqrt{t}\\,⇒\\,t={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t={x}^{2}$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={x}^{2}(9-{x}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y={x}^{2}(9-{x}^{2}).$\n\nFinally, since $t\\ge 0$ and $x=\\sqrt{t},$ we must have $x\\ge 0.$\n\nSo, the complete Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\ny={x}^{2}(9-{x}^{2}),\\,x\\ge 0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275355 :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-3,\\,\\,\\,y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-3\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=x+3 \\\\ y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t=1$, we get\n\n$$\n(x+3)^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $(x+3)^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275366 :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically by\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{t+1},\\,\\,\\,y=\\frac{1}{t-2},\\,t\\ne -1,\\,2.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{1}{t+1}\\,⇒\\,t=\\frac{1}{x}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow substituting $t=\\frac{1}{x}-1$ into our expression for $y$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{((\\frac{1}{x}-1)-2)} \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{x}-3)} \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{1-3x}{x}\\right)} \\\\ y & =\\frac{x}{1-3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=\\frac{x}{1-3x}.$\n\nFinally, since $t\\ne -1,\\,2$ and $x=\\frac{1}{t+1},$ we must have $x\\ne 0,\\,\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\nSo, the complete Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{x}{1-3x},\\,x\\ne 0,\\,\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275367 :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2,\\,\\,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+3,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=x+2 \\\\ y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+3\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=y-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n(y-3)^{2}+(x+2)^{2}=1.\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $(y-3)^{2}+(x+2)^{2}=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275401 :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,0<t<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=x \\\\ y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡t=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity ${\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t & =1 \\\\ {\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t & =1-{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}t & =\\sqrt{1-{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe used here that $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t>0$ in the range $0<t<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nNow, using the definition of a tangent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}t=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}t}{\\operatorname{cos}t}=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}t}{\\sqrt{1-{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t}}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=x$ and $\\operatorname{tan}⁡t=y$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275402 :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cot}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡t,\\,t\\in (0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=3\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{cot}⁡t=\\frac{x}{3} \\\\ y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡t=y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the Pythagorean identity ${\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t=1$ by ${\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t}{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t}+\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}t}{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t} & =\\frac{1}{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}t} \\\\ 1+{\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}t}{\\operatorname{sin}t}\\right)}^{2} & ={\\left(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}t}\\right)}^{2} \\\\ 1+{\\operatorname{cot}}^{2}t & ={\\operatorname{csc}}^{2}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cot}⁡t=\\frac{x}{3}$ and $\\operatorname{csc}⁡t=y$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+{\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right)}^{2}={y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is ${y}^{2}=1+{\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right)}^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275403 :question/problem "Determine the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{5} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{5}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{25} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{y}{3} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275404 :question/problem "Determine the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy squaring the $x$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\ {x}^{2} & =(2\\operatorname{cos}\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & =4{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy squaring the $y$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\ {y}^{2} & =(2\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & =4{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =4{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +4{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta  \\\\  & =4({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta ) \\\\  & =4(1) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275413 :question/problem "Which of the following parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate a circle of radius $5$ centered at the origin?" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=5.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are \n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275453 :question/problem "Find the domain of the function $z=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}.$" :question/worked-solution "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n- The expression in the denominator is not equal to zero, and\n\n- the expression under the square root is non-negative.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}>0\\,⟹\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {\\mathbb{R}}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}:\\hspace{0.167em}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<1\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275454 :question/problem "Which of the following shaded regions corresponds to the domain of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}?$" :question/worked-solution "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following condition is satisfied:\n\n- The expression under the square root is non-negative, and\n\n- the expression in the denominator is not equal to zero.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequalities:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}>0\\,⟹\\,{y}^{2}<{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,-|x|<y<|x|\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\in {\\mathbb{R}}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}:\\hspace{0.167em}-|x|<y<|x|\\}.\n$$\n\nSince the inequality involves the $<$ sign, the curves are *not* part of the domain. Therefore, we draw the dashed lines $y=-|x|$ and $y=|x|,$ and shade the area *below* the curve $y=|x|,$ and *above* the curve $y=-|x|.$\n\nThe resulting domain is as follows:\n\n![](images/c0/c06082999e1737b6e4ea5d32a3d8d77a271e3db49c38cc3725422bcb979676d0.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275455 :question/problem "Which of the following shaded regions corresponds to the domain of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "The function $f(x,y)$ is defined for all points $(x,y)$ on the plane, hence the domain is the entire $xy$-plane. \n\n![](images/40/402d90507403eabf4cd35cde83a45c9fa2d8080347e1debb0e2463be408b489b.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275456 :question/problem "Which of the following shaded regions corresponds to the domain of the function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}?$" :question/worked-solution "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following condition is satisfied:\n\n- The expression under the square root is non-negative.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequalities:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\ge 0\\,⟹\\,{y}^{2}\\le {x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,-|x|\\le y\\le |x|\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\in {\\mathbb{R}}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}:\\hspace{0.167em}-|x|\\le y\\le |x|\\}.\n$$\n\nSince the inequality involves the $\\le $ sign, the curves are part of the domain. Therefore, we draw the solid curves $y=-|x|$ and $y=|x|,$ and shade the area *below* the curve $y=|x|,$ and *above* the curve $y=-|x|.$\n\nThe resulting domain is as follows:\n\n![](images/95/95f187d2857973e4fea83eedf47bd54c153ce2a77f278cfb992b222de2d91ab8.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278183 :question/problem "Determine the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{6} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{6}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{36} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{y}{2} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278192 :question/problem "Determine the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1,\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-1 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\ 2(x-1) & =\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\ 4(x-1)^{2} & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-2 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\ 2(y-2) & ={\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta  \\\\ 4(y-2)^{2} & ={\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(x-1)^{2}+4(y-2)^{2} & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta  \\\\ 4(x-1)^{2}+4(y-2)^{2} & =1 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence the Cartesian equation of the circle is $(x-1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=\\frac{1}{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278204 :question/problem "Find the domain of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{2x+y}.$" :question/worked-solution "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following condition is satisfied:\n\n- The expression in the denominator is not equal to zero.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequality:\n\n$$\n2x+y\\ne 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {\\mathbb{R}}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}:\\hspace{0.167em}2x+y\\ne 0\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278205 :question/problem "Find the domain of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{x+{y}^{3}}{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following condition is satisfied:\n\n- The expression in the denominator is not equal to zero.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequality:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-1\\ne 0\\,⟹\\,{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\ne 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {\\mathbb{R}}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}:\\hspace{0.167em}{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\ne 1\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278206 :question/problem "Find the domain of the function $z=\\frac{\\sqrt{x-1}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n- The expression in the denominator is not equal to zero, and\n\n- the expression under the square root is non-negative.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequalities:\n\n$$\n\\begin{cases}x-1\\ge 0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-1\\ne 0\\end{cases}\\,⟹\\,\\begin{cases}x\\ge 1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 1\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in \\mathbb{R}^{2}\\,:\\,x\\ge 1,\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 1\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278207 :question/problem "Find the domain of the function $z=\\frac{\\sqrt{x-y}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4}}.$" :question/worked-solution "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n- The expression in the denominator is not equal to zero, and\n\n- the expression under the square root is non-negative.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequalities:\n\n$$\n\\begin{cases}x-y\\ge 0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4>0\\end{cases}\\,⟹\\,\\begin{cases}x\\ge y \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>4\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in \\mathbb{R}^{2}\\,:\\,x\\ge y,\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>4\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278209 :question/problem "Find the domain of the function $z=\\frac{\\sqrt{2y}}{\\sqrt{2-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}.$" :question/worked-solution "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n- The expression in the denominator is not equal to zero, and\n\n- the expression under the square root is non-negative.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequalities:\n\n$$\n\\begin{cases}2y\\ge 0 \\\\ 2-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}>0\\end{cases}\\,⟹\\,\\begin{cases}y\\ge 0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<2\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in \\mathbb{R}^{2}\\,:\\,y\\ge 0,\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<2\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278210 :question/problem "Find the domain of the function $f(x,y)={e}^{{x}^{2}/y}.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n- $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}$ is defined for $x$ and $y,$ such that $y\\ne 0,$ and\n\n- ${e}^{g(x,y)}$ is defined if $g(x,y)$ is defined.\n\nAs a result, ${e}^{{x}^{2}/y}$ is defined for values of $x$ and $y$ that satisfy $y\\ne 0.$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $f$ is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {\\mathbb{R}}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}:\\hspace{0.167em}y\\ne 0\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278211 :question/problem "Evaluate $z=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x-2y+4)$ at the point $(-4,1).$" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate our multivariable function at the point $(-4,1),$ we substitute $x=-4$ and $y=1$ into the function expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z\\,{|}_{(-4,1)} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x-2y+4)\\,{|}_{(-4,1)} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3(-4)-2(1)+4) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-12-2+4) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-10),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is undefined."}]}
